## Session 7 summary

**Five builds landed and validated (0.11.21 → 0.11.27):**

1. **Rescue-clock honesty (0.11.22)** — the resource-scoped starve/orphan clocks were never cleared by successful releases, so every contended dir drew a spurious gen-blind force-unlock every 3s per node (72/run measured, some mid-ACQUIRING). Proven by a force firing 0.4s after a successful release; now cleared only on real wire unlocks.
2. **MHT quiet-age gate + grace=40 (0.11.23/26)** — idle tenures used to sleep out the full 300ms batching floor because the deferred-BAST dwork was armed for the whole window. Tenures now release once unconsumed ≥ grace. Result: **first-ever sub-60s cc@32 (56s)**, and 8/16-node got *faster* across the board (cc@8 25→11s, cc@16 31→25s, all rows green — regression check passed).
3. **The 470s ABBA wedge chain dissolved (0.11.24-26)** — captured live and diagnosed to four stacked defects (cluster release_check success-lie disarming the rescue, frozen grant-gen parking all CAW BASTs, and a sticky-routing conversion leaking unreachable per-ino EX bits). Fixed per a GPT RULE-5 design ruling (honest rc, gg-consultation TCP-only, drain-safe per-ino reconcile gated to entry-orphan pipelines). Zero wedges in all subsequent runs; the stall class (>2s waits) is completely gone.

**The frontier, now measured to the millisecond (0.11.27 anatomy prints):** cc@32 sits at 56-80s against a hard 60s bar, functionally perfect every run. The rotation anatomy refuted the tenure-chop theory — batching is excellent (40-60 ops/tenure). The two real terms, both quantified in state.md PART-5 with instrumentation recipes: **113ms-p50/1s-p90 dead time between tenures** (should be ≤5ms given the verified ticket+nudge machinery), and **3 lock cycles per rename** (the revalidate-PR→EX-upgrade→op pattern) tripling rotations per phase. After cc: close_release TTL defaults, drc's round pipeline, fairness@32, the remaining 32 rows, then the tcp/cawp/caw ladders and the final matrix.

